Radiography is used extensively in oil refineries to monitor equipment integrity. For example, radiography can measure corrosion of a pipe or find weld defects.

The types of radiography can be roughly grouped into conventional and digital.

Conventional radiography (the incumbent technology) uses a radiation source and film to capture images. Copies of the physical film are stored as back-up.

Digital radiography (specifically computed radiography) uses imaging plate coated with a phosphor material. [1] Digital Radiography (DR) is a newer technology, and adoption is increasing.

Benefits of DR include:

✅ Faster Imaging

✅ Enhanced Image Quality

✅ Digital Records

✅ Smaller Exclusion Zones [2]

The smaller exclusion zones should not be overlooked. In a refinery, limiting the exclusion zone can increase productivity, minimizing the impact on Operations and other maintenance activities.

Basically, more work can get done because the area around the radiography that must be barricaded is minimized.

